WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
Every September, our deputy head

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:05.130
spends a week with a pencil and an

3
00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.230
eraser, building the timetable.

4
00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:10.570
Then one teacher changes her

5
00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:12.530
days, and it all falls apart.

6
00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:16.090
What if we just told a computer the rules?

7
00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.670
Today: a timetable that works,

8
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.770
written as a model in Python.

9
00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.140
A timetable is a model like any other.

10
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.840
The options: which period

11
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.920
each lesson goes in.

12
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.820
The limits: a class has one

13
00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.360
lesson at a time, and a teacher

14
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.260
is in one class at a time.

15
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.320
There’s no goal yet: any timetable

16
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.140
that keeps every rule will do.

17
00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:46.600
In Google’s OR-Tools, each lesson gets a

18
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.500
variable for its period, from one to four.

19
00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.140
One rule says a class’s lessons

20
00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:53.980
are all in different periods.

21
00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.360
Another says the same for each subject,

22
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:00.400
because each subject here has one teacher.

23
00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:03.540
Real schools add rules.

24
00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:06.510
Ms Huda leaves after period three.

25
00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.880
Six A’s maths test is in period one.

26
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.600
Each rule is one more line.

27
00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.370
How does the solver find a timetable?

28
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.950
First it crosses out periods that can’t

29
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.390
work: if six A’s maths is in period one,

30
00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.970
Mr Musa can’t teach anyone else then.

31
00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:27.090
That’s propagation.

32
00:01:28.070 --> 00:01:29.570
When nothing more can be crossed

33
00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:32.670
out, it guesses, and undoes any

34
00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.230
guess that leads to a dead end.

35
00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:38.270
In the lab’s small school, propagation

36
00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.670
and search need eight guesses.

37
00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.490
Plain backtracking, guessing

38
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:45.950
without crossing out, needs three

39
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.230
hundred and eighty-nine.

40
00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.270
Good rules save work.

41
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:51.130
Quick check!

42
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.660
I add one more rule, and the

43
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.800
solver says INFEASIBLE.

44
00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:58.830
What does that tell me?

45
00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.950
No timetable keeps all these

46
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.150
rules: the solver has proved it;

47
00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:05.950
it hasn’t just given up.

48
00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:09.990
In the lab, add “the science lab is busy

49
00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.450
in period one” too: science needs three

50
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.370
periods, and only two are left.

51
00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:18.910
So talk to people: which rule can bend?

52
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.670
A timetable that keeps the

53
00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.470
rules can still be unkind.

54
00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:26.310
Does one class get every hard

55
00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:27.470
subject after lunch?

56
00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.480
Does Ms Rana sit through

57
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.500
a gap all morning?

58
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:32.660
Who gets the last period of

59
00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:33.740
the week, every week?

60
00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:35.630
Check the result with the

61
00:02:35.630 --> 00:02:36.690
people who live with it.

62
00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.980
In the app, open “Build the timetable”.

63
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.680
Press One step, and watch the

64
00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.440
rules cross periods out.

65
00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.680
Then press Run, switch to plain

66
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.820
backtracking, and compare the guesses.

67
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:52.930
Your turn.

68
00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.090
The solver found a timetable, but five B

69
00:02:57.090 --> 00:03:00.010
has maths in the last period every day.

70
00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.340
What do you do?

71
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.910
Add a rule and solve again: the

72
00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:05.950
model makes that cheap.

73
00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.140
Remember: periods are variables,

74
00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.240
rules are lines, and the solver

75
00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.900
crosses out and searches.

76
00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:17.110
INFEASIBLE means the rules clash.

77
00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.670
And a timetable that fits

78
00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:21.490
still needs fair eyes.

79
00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.260
Try it in class tomorrow: draw a

80
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.000
timetable grid on the board, with

81
00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.280
sticky notes for the lessons.

82
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:30.710
Pupils place them, and

83
00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.050
call out every clash.

84
00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:35.840
Then add one new rule, and see

85
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.120
how much has to move.
